<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Duck Lake</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">26 March <a href="/years/1885">1885</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · <a href="/today-in-history/march-26">On this day: 26 March</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-west-rebellion">North-West Rebellion</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Duck Lake was fought on 26 March 1885 near Duck Lake in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saskatchewan">Saskatchewan</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a> and First Nations fighters under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabriel-dumont">Gabriel Dumont</a> defeated a force of North-West Mounted Police and volunteers led by Superintendent Leif Crozier. Twelve of the government force were killed. The clash began the armed phase of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-west-rebellion">North-West Resistance</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-duck-lake.jpg" alt="Battle of Duck Lake" width="320" height="232" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Believed to be from The Canadian Pictorial &amp; Illustrated War News, a souvenir number published on 1885-07-04, or The Illustrated … · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Duck_Lake.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>On 19 March 1885 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-riel">Louis Riel</a> proclaimed a Provisional Government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saskatchewan">Saskatchewan</a>, and the Canadian government moved to reassert its control. Crozier, newly appointed to command the North-West Mounted Police in north-western Saskatchewan, asked for reinforcements at Fort Carlton. Riel sent emissaries demanding the surrender of the fort without bloodshed; Crozier's representatives refused and vowed that the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a> leaders would be brought to justice.</p><p>On 25 March Crozier sent Sergeant Alfred Stewart, Thomas McKay and seventeen constables to fetch supplies from Hillyard Mitchell's store at Duck Lake. On the morning of the 26th they met <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gabriel-dumont">Gabriel Dumont</a>'s men, already entrenched on the road, and turned back without a shot. Crozier then set out himself with 53 police, 41 men of the Prince Albert Volunteers and a 7-pounder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The forces met about 2.5 kilometres outside Duck Lake on a snowy plateau with trees, shrubs and a few log cabins, where Dumont's men had taken cover. Dumont sent his brother Isidore and an elderly half-blind chief, Assiwiyin, forward under a white flag. Crozier, believing they were stalling while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a> moved round his flank, ordered his interpreter, Joe McKay, to fire when the two appeared to draw their guns, and both were killed.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/metis">Métis</a> were more numerous and well placed in the cabins and tree line. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> was put out of action when a shell was loaded before its powder charge, and within half an hour Crozier ordered a retreat to Fort Carlton. Riel stopped his men from pursuing. Eleven of the government force were seriously wounded; five Métis were killed, and Dumont was injured in the head by a passing bullet.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>A council called by Colonel Acheson Irvine decided to abandon Fort Carlton, and the last sleigh left the burning fort by 4 am on 28 March. Historians generally judge the battle a tactical success but a strategic disappointment for Riel, who had hoped to take Crozier and his men hostage to force the government's hand. The site was designated a national historic site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> in 1924, and a First Shots Cairn on Saskatchewan Highway 212 marks where the fighting began.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Duck Lake” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Métis fighters defeated the North-West Mounted Police at <b>Duck Lake</b> on 26 March, while Cree warriors from Big Bear's and Poundmaker's bands fought separately at Frog Lake, Battleford and Cut Knife.</blockquote><a href="/state/provisional-government-of-saskatchewan">Provisional Government of Saskatchewan</a></li></ul></section>
